From 3824e0d9968ae9cc4f7809f385615541ed176120 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 22:01:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] virtio-gpu: enable async fencing on the render-server
 path without EGL

Upstream gates async fencing behind qemu_egl_display because vrend's
async path needs EGL.  The render-server proxy does not: it retires
fences on its own sync thread, fed by an eventfd/pipe the render worker
writes per retire.  On a macOS Cocoa/Metal build qemu_egl_display is
NULL, so the feature was inert and the only retire discovery for
venus/neptune fences was the 1 ms fence_poll timer.  Install the async
callbacks and pass ASYNC_FENCE_CB | THREAD_SYNC whenever the render
server is in use (virglrenderer scopes the flags away from vrend where
it lacks eventfd support).

The async BH now delegates to virgl_write_fence() /
virgl_write_context_fence() instead of carrying its own matching loop:
upstream's loop let a context fence complete a global fenced cmd with a
numerically smaller id, but global and per-context fence ids are
separate counters here, and the strict handlers also keep per-cmd
inflight accounting.

fence_poll stays as a watchdog: while any global (non-ring) fence is
pending it keeps the 1 ms rate -- vrend fences still retire only from
virgl_renderer_poll() -- and relaxes to 100 ms when only ring fences
(async-delivered) remain.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
---
 hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c  | 100 ++++++++++++++++++++++-----------
 include/hw/virtio/virtio-gpu.h |   2 +
