From 161407f4c52bffab32d4aed03d39488f835e8973 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: osy <osy@turing.llc>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 13:33:39 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] arm/hvf: emulate a full PMUv3 so guests get a sampling
 profiler

Apple silicon has no architected PMUv3 (ID_AA64DFR0_EL1.PMUVer == 0), so
guests had no CPU sampling source: Windows on Arm refuses ETW
sampled-profile sessions with ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED and Linux perf finds
no PMU.

Advertise base PMUv3 (PMUVer = 1) with six event counters
(reset_pmcr_el0 = 6 << PMCRN_SHIFT; IMP/IDCODE left 0 like the TCG CPUs
that don't model a specific implementation) and emulate the whole unit:

 - Replace hvf.c's partial hand-rolled PMU register emulation (which had
   drifted from helper.c: PMINTENCLR_EL1 set instead of clearing,
   PMINTENSET_EL1/PMOVSSET_EL0 UNDEFed, PMCEID read as zero, no event
   counters) with hvf_sysreg_access_pmu(), which routes every PMU
   register through the cpreg handlers helper.c registers.  This shares
   TCG's implementation of PMUSERENR_EL0 access control (EL0 denial
   raises the architectural trap, mirroring access_check_cp_reg()),
   per-counter op_start/finish bracketing, the pre-FEAT_PMUv3p5 RES0
   masking of event counter reads, the c14_pmevcntr_delta re-baseline on
   event type changes, and PMCEID0/1 derived from pm_events.
 - Refresh env->pstate from HV_REG_CPSR on EC_SYSTEMREGISTERTRAP
   (hvf_sync_pstate()) so arm_current_el() is accurate for the cpreg
   access checks instead of stuck at its reset value.
 - Under HVF the vCPU runs unobserved between traps, so PMEVTYPER.P/U
   cannot be honoured per-EL, and evaluating them at trap-time EL would
   permanently disable e.g. perf's cycles:u (the registers are only
   touched from EL1): pmu_counter_enabled() now counts unless the filter
   excludes every EL, leaving sample attribution to the interrupted PC.
 - pmcr_write() re-evaluates the interrupt line when PMCR_EL0.E changes
   (per CheckForPMUOverflow(); TCG had the same latent gap), and a
   per-CPU reset deasserts the level-triggered PPI, which nothing
   lowered across a CPU_OFF/CPU_ON cycle since a single-CPU reset does
   not reset the GICv3.
 - Add a cpu/pmu-sysregs vmstate subsection carrying the raw PMU fields
   (with the pmevtyper_rawwrite()-style delta re-baseline on load): HVF
   rebuilds the cpreg list from the registers the hypervisor can sync,
   which excludes the emulated PMU, so its state silently reset across
   savevm/loadvm.
 - ID_AA64DFR0_EL1 needs no manual sync: it is in hvf_sreg_match[], so
   hvf_put_registers() pushes the model's value before the first run,
   and pmu=off keeps reporting PMUVer == 0 since realize zeroes the isar
   field (Windows reads PMCCNTR_EL0 even then, so it cannot boot with
   the PMU off; noted at the site).

The helper.c machinery (virtual-clock cycle counts, pmu_timer overflow,
PPI 23 already wired on the virt board and advertised in the MADT) does
the rest.

Verified: Ubuntu boots, registers armv8_pmuv3 with 6 counters, perf stat
counts cycles at the virtual clock rate, perf record -e cycles and
cycles:u both deliver samples with overflow IRQs matching sample counts,
an EL0 PMCCNTR_EL0 read with PMUSERENR_EL0 == 0 takes SIGILL, and Venus
comes up.  Windows 11 arm64 boots and wpr -start CPU records real
SampledProfile events with stacks (previously 0x80070032).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
